WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 139: Abraham Lincoln and the Cooper Union Speech: Right Makes Might

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.480
[Rebecca] Today we begin our exploration of America's 16th president, Abraham Lincoln.

4
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:23.280
[Rebecca] Before he was the great emancipator, before he carried the nation through the crucible of civil war, Lincoln was an unpolished frontier lawyer with a high-pitched voice and a wrinkled suit.

5
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:35.840
[Rebecca] In February 1860, he walked into the heart of New York City's intellectual elite and delivered a speech of such transcendent moral and legal power that it changed the course of American history.

6
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.680
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the Cooper Union Address.

7
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:49.840
[George] In early 1860, the frontrunner for the Republican nomination for President of the United States seemed unshakable.

8
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.440
[George] Senator William H.

9
00:00:51.760 --> 00:01:03.600
[George] Seward of New York, former governor, brilliant orator, and champion of the higher law against slavery, commanded the wealthy, powerful New York political machine directed by Thurlow Weed.

10
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.480
[George] To Eastern elites, the next president of the United States was already chosen.

11
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:17.680
[George] A thousand miles to the west, in Springfield, Illinois, 51-year-old Abraham Lincoln was quietly daring to dream an impossible dream.

12
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.920
[George] On paper, Lincoln's political credentials were microscopic.

13
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:29.840
[George] Four terms in the Illinois State Legislature and a single, undistinguished two-year term in the House of Representatives twelve years earlier.

14
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.160
[George] He had run for the United States Senate twice and lost both times.

15
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.840
[George] His fame rested entirely on his losing 1858 campaign against the Titan Stephen A.

16
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.320
[George] Douglas, whose debates had been published in newspapers across the country.

17
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] In October 1859, Lincoln received an unexpected letter from a young men's Republican committee in New York, inviting him to deliver a lecture as part of a winter lecture series at Henry Ward Beecher's Plymouth Church in Brooklyn, offering a speaking fee of $200.

18
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.160
[George] Lincoln accepted instantly.

19
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:14.760
[George] He knew that if he was ever to be considered a serious dark horse contender for the national ticket, he had to prove himself before the sophisticated, critical literary and political establishment of New York.

20
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:20.760
[George] Abraham Lincoln did not prepare for New York by writing applause lines or fiery stump slogans.

21
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:27.400
[George] He approached the assignment like a supreme constitutional advocate, preparing a brief for the highest court in the land.

22
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:38.360
[George] For three grueling months, during the freezing Illinois winter of 1859 and 60, Lincoln virtually lived inside the Illinois State Capitol Law Library in Springfield.

23
00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:49.320
[George] He called for trunk after trunk of colonial records, congressional journals, the Northwest Ordinance of 1787, and the secret debates of the Constitutional Convention.

24
00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:57.720
[George] He meticulously tabulated the individual voting records of every single one of the 39 men who had signed the United States Constitution.

25
00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:04.440
[George] Lincoln's objective was to answer a central, deceitful claim made by Southern Democrats and Stephen Douglas.

26
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:17.400
[George] Douglas had repeatedly claimed that the Founding Fathers had established the principle of popular sovereignty, believing that the federal government had no constitutional right to restrict or prohibit slavery in federal territories.

27
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.200
[George] Lincoln was determined to test that claim against hard, historical evidence.

28
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:33.400
[George] He worked by candlelight late into the night, compiling names, dates, roll call tallies, and presidential signatures, constructing an airtight, mathematical fortress of fact.

29
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:46.640
[George] On February 25, 1860, after a grueling three-day train journey across snowbound tracks, Abraham Lincoln arrived in New York City, checking into the elegant Astore House Hotel on Broadway.

30
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.640
[George] Lincoln was an alarming, awkward sight in the fashionable metropolis.

31
00:03:50.880 --> 00:04:04.080
[George] He stood six feet four inches tall, a towering giant in an era when the average man stood five feet seven, with long, dangling arms, enormous hands, hollow cheeks, and deep, melancholy grey eyes.

32
00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:10.960
[George] To prepare for his Eastern debut, Lincoln had purchased a brand new suit of black broadcloth in Springfield.

33
00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:20.880
[George] But the suit had been packed into his leather traveling valise, and when he unpacked it at the Astore House, the coat and trousers were a mass of heavy creases and wrinkles.

34
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:29.760
[George] Even worse, the tailor had cut the sleeves too short, exposing Lincoln's large bony wrists, and the trousers rode high above his boots.

35
00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.160
[George] Furthermore, Lincoln learned upon arrival that his venue had been moved.

36
00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:48.320
[George] Because of intense public interest, the lecture had been relocated from Beecher's Brooklyn Church to the Great Hall of the Cooper Union for the advancement of science and art in Manhattan, a massive underground auditorium seating 1,500 people.

37
00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.160
[George] Lincoln was terrified.

38
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:58.080
[George] He was an unpolished frontier lawyer about to speak before the most sophisticated, cynical, and educated audience in America.

39
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:08.720
[George] On the freezing, snow-covered evening of Monday, February 27, 1860, 1,500 people packed the subterranean Great Hall of the Cooper Union.

40
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.880
[George] Admission was 25 cents.

41
00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:16.080
[George] In the audience sat New York's cultural, financial, and intellectual royalty.

42
00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:29.960
[George] Prominent judges, Wall Street bankers, editors, and scholars, including the venerable poet William Cullen Bryant, editor of the New York Evening Post, and Horace Greeley, the influential editor of the New York Tribune.

43
00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:36.280
[George] When William Cullen Bryant introduced Lincoln, a ripple of uneasy skepticism passed through the crowd.

44
00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:40.280
[George] Lincoln stepped to the podium looking awkward and uncomfortable.

45
00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:48.920
[George] His wrinkled black suit hung loosely on his frame, his hair was unruly, and one of his stiff white shirt collars rode high on his neck.

46
00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:55.000
[George] An eyewitness in the audience wrote that Lincoln looked like a backwoods rube who had wandered into an opera house.

47
00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.160
[George] When Lincoln began to speak, the initial impression grew worse.

48
00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:07.240
[George] His voice was high-pitched, thin, and carried a sharp, distinctive frontier Kentucky twang that grated on refined eastern ears.

49
00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:13.400
[George] He seemed nervous, his hands shaking slightly as he adjusted his manuscript on the wooden lectern.

50
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:21.400
[George] Many in the audience looked at one another in disappointment, wondering how this ungainly westerner had ever dared challenge Stephen Douglas.

51
00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:26.920
[George] But then, as Lincoln warmed to his argument, a miraculous transformation occurred.

52
00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:30.520
[George] Within five minutes, Lincoln's awkwardness vanished.

53
00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:43.720
[George] His stooped posture straightened until he towered above the crowd, his gray eyes lit up with incandescent moral fire, and his voice deepened into a resonant, booming baritone that reached the farthest corner of the cavernous hall.

54
00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.760
[George] The great hall fell into spellbound silence.

55
00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:50.360
[George] Lincoln took as his text a quote from Stephen Douglas.

56
00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:58.840
[George] Than we do now.

57
00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:06.360
[George] Lincoln agreed with the premise, and then proceeded to systematically dismantle Douglas with his three months of library research.

58
00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:11.880
[George] With mathematical precision, Lincoln examined the thirty-nine signers of the Constitution.

59
00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:27.200
[George] He proved that of the thirty-nine framers, twenty-one of them, an absolute majority, had voted on federal measures that restricted or prohibited slavery in federal territories, including George Washington, Thomas Jefferson, and James Madison.

60
00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:33.280
[George] In total, thirty-one of the thirty-nine had supported federal control over territorial slavery.

61
00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:38.640
[George] With devastating, surgical logic, Lincoln turned Douglas's argument against him.

62
00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:45.920
[George] The Founding Fathers, Lincoln proved, had never intended slavery to be an eternal, sacred national institution.

63
00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:53.040
[George] They had treated it as an evil to be tolerated where it existed, but strictly contained and marked for ultimate extinction.

64
00:07:53.040 --> 00:08:02.000
[George] The audience, accustomed to flowery rhetorical bombs, was mesmerized by Lincoln's calm, relentless mastery of constitutional history.

65
00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:06.560
[George] In the second half of his address, Lincoln turned his attention to the South.

66
00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:12.080
[George] Speaking with profound, dispassionate calm, he addressed Southern threats of disunion.

67
00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:20.480
[George] Southern radicals had warned that if a Republican was elected president, the South would secede and blame the North for the destruction of the Union.

68
00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:25.520
[George] Lincoln exposed the moral absurdity of this threat with a brilliant frontier parable.

69
00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.160
[George] A highwayman holds a pistol to my ear.

70
00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:34.800
[George] And mutters through his teeth, Stand and deliver, or I shall kill you, and then you will be a murderer.

71
00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:40.320
[George] The threat was not my threat, but his, and the crime was not my crime, but his.

72
00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:44.960
[George] Lincoln declared that the South would never be satisfied with Northern silence.

73
00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:48.960
[George] They demanded that the North declare slavery to be morally right.

74
00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.240
[George] Then came the magnificent, thunderous climax.

75
00:08:52.240 --> 00:09:00.920
[George] Gathering his full height, his voice ringing with prophetic moral power, Lincoln delivered the words that would echo down the corridors of American memory.

76
00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:11.320
[George] Neither let us be slandered from our duty by false accusations against us, nor frightened from it by menaces of destruction to the government, nor of dungeons to ourselves.

77
00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.920
[George] Let us have faith that right makes might, and in that faith, let us, to the end, dare to do our duty as we understand it.

78
00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:22.760
[George] For a heartbeat, the hall was frozen in stunned silence.

79
00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.880
[George] Then, 1,500 people leaped to their feet.

80
00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:35.400
[George] Men threw their hats into the air, women waved white handkerchiefs, and the great hall erupted in a deafening, standing ovation that lasted for ten minutes.

81
00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:43.400
[George] Horace Greeley leaned toward a colleague and declared, No man has made such an impression in New York since the days of Daniel Webster.

82
00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:46.760
[George] The Cooper Union address was a political earthquake.

83
00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:53.960
[George] The following morning, four major New York Daily newspapers printed the full text of Lincoln's speech on their front pages.

84
00:09:53.960 --> 00:10:04.920
[George] Over the next month, over 100,000 pamphlet copies were printed and distributed across the northern states, read aloud at town halls and firesides from Maine to Minnesota.

85
00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:12.280
[George] Almost overnight, Lincoln was transformed from an obscure Illinois prairie lawyer into a first-rank national statesman.

86
00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:15.880
[George] He demonstrated that he possessed what frontrunner William H.

87
00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:24.840
[George] Seward lacked, a dispassionate, unassailable conservative appeal rooted in the Constitution, paired with an unyielding moral core.

88
00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:36.360
[George] Three months later, in May 1860, ten thousand delegates and spectators packed the vast wooden convention hall known as the Wigwam in Chicago for the Republican National Convention.

89
00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:43.880
[George] Through masterly maneuvering by his campaign team, Lincoln's forces held Seward below a majority on the first two ballots.

90
00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:52.880
[George] On the third ballot, the dam broke, state after state swung to Lincoln, and Abraham Lincoln was nominated for President of the United States.

91
00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:58.000
[George] Lincoln later acknowledged that the Cooper Union speech was the turning point of his life.

92
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:03.840
[George] He told friends, that speech made me president in a single night in Manhattan.

93
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:17.680
[George] An unpolished frontier lawyer with wrinkled clothes and a high-pitched voice had proved that in a great republic, true power belongs not to wealth, pedigree, or political machines, but to the unshakable force of moral truth.

94
00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:23.120
[George] Wright had made might, and the man who would save the Union was on his way to history.

95
00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:32.000
[Rebecca] Abraham Lincoln's Cooper Union address launched his improbable journey to the White House and defined the moral spine of the American Republic.

96
00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:45.120
[Rebecca] Next week, we continue with Abraham Lincoln's second colossal chapter The Crucible of the Civil War, the drafting of the Emancipation Proclamation, and the Immortal Consecration of the Battlefield at Gettysburg.

97
00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:49.520
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

